List only the settings a browser can actually open

v4.190.1 shipped this patch listing six sections in the tile's settings nav
whose panes `Settings.tsx` does not render. Clicking Orca Account, Mobile,
Browser, SSH Hosts, Computer Use or Plugins in the tile landed on a blank page.
Confirmed on the deployed 4.190.1 workspace: each nav row is present, and the
pane area holds the sidebar and nothing else.

The cause is that the decision lives in two files. This patch changed
`useSettingsNavigationMetadata.ts`, which builds the NAV LIST. It did not touch
`Settings.tsx`, which RENDERS the panes behind its own copy of
`showDesktopOnlySettings = !isWebClient`. Every section the nav opened was still
wrapped in that gate, so the list and the page disagreed and nothing checked
them against each other. Hidden was honest; listed-and-empty makes the UI lie.

Both files now answer the same way, and `settings-nav-render-parity.test.ts`
reads them together so they cannot drift again: it scans the JSX gates in
`Settings.tsx` and fails on any id the web nav lists without a pane behind it.

Three sections are listed AND rendered, each checked against the deployed host
rather than against the RPC registry:

- Computer Use. On a non-Mac host the pane's own `platform !== 'darwin'` check
  drops the macOS permission block, leaving the Computer Use skill setup panel,
  which runs on `skills.discover` (real host data) and a working terminal. The
  nav's install badge is ungated with it, so the row and the pane agree.
- Mobile. `pairing-credentials.diff` wires mint and revoke for this pane, and
  its "To test" names Settings > Mobile.
- Browser. Its rows are settings-backed through `settings.update`, and
  `browser.screencast.v1` is advertised by `orca serve` on the host.

Five stay out of the web nav, because their panes cannot tell the truth yet:

- Orca Account. `orcaProfiles.authStatus` answers `configured: true` on the
  host, which ENABLES a "Sign in to Orca" button that can never succeed - sign-in
  is measured but not built (`docs/audit/v1.4.190/N3-orca-account-sign-in.md`),
  and the host session would be memory-only with no keyring. An enabled sign-in
  button implies sign-in works.
- Plugins. `plugins.list` answers `[]` and `pluginSystemEnabled` is false;
  `install`, `refresh` and `remove` are not routed, so `install` throws on
  `result.ok` of an undefined. There is no host CLI route either, so the list
  can never become non-empty from the tile.
- SSH Hosts. `ssh.listTargets` answers `[]`, `addTarget` rejects,
  `removeTarget` silently resolves, `importConfig` returns empty and
  `testConnection` is a hardcoded failure. Every route to a first target is a
  stub, so the pane is permanently empty.
- Mobile Emulator and macOS Permissions. The earlier host-platform axis
  (`hostPlatform` from `status.get`) is removed: it listed both to a browser
  driving a Mac host, and neither pane was ever exercised over the wire. Their
  gate is upstream's again.

Two claims the earlier header made are withdrawn. `computer.capabilities` is
called by no renderer file - only by that header - so listing Computer Use never
surfaced its missing-package message. And listing a section because its RPC
family is registered is not evidence the pane works: `ssh` and `plugins` both
have registered families and unusable panes.

Voice, Notifications and Advanced stay hidden for the reasons recorded before:
no `speech` namespace in the web preload, native toasts through an inert host
port, and proxy keys the `SettingsUpdate` schema rejects.

Consequence worth stating: `plugins-web-bridge.diff`'s "To test, open Settings >
Plugins in the tile" is unfollowable again. That patch wired the read half only,
and this patch is not the place to finish it.

To test, open Settings in the tile: Computer Use, Mobile and Browser are listed
and each opens onto a rendered pane; Orca Account, Plugins and SSH Hosts are not
listed, and searching for "plugin" or "orca account" finds nothing. On the
desktop every section is listed exactly as before. Covered by
`settings-nav-web-parity.test.ts`, which pins the web and desktop nav both ways,
and `settings-nav-render-parity.test.ts`, which fails if the nav lists a section
`Settings.tsx` does not render.
